   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT providing for the appeal and review of certain real property tax\nexemption applications",
    "basePrintNo": "S7175",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7175-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7175\n\nSPONSOR: WEIK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act providing for the appeal and review of certain real property tax\nexemption applications\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo allow certain non-profit organizations to commence a Board of Assess-\nment Review challenge relating to the 2020 and 2021 assessment roll\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one authorizes non-profit organizations in Suffolk County who\napplied for the 420-a or 420-b exemption on the 2020 and 2021 assessment\nroll in a timely fashion, and such application was denied, to commence a\nBoard of Assessment Review challenge within 30 days of the effective\ndate of this act if such organization failed to bring an initial\ncomplaint to the Board of Assessment Review. Such organization is\nauthorized to appeal the determination made by the Board of Assessment\npursuant to this legislation in a tax certiorari proceeding should they\nchoose to do so.\n\nSection two is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDuring the Covid-19 pandemic many institutions of higher education were\nclosed and struggling to maintain normal operations. This legislation\nwill ensure that any school that was closed and missed their opportunity\nto appeal denials of their tax exemptions are able to exercise their due\nprocess that is normally available to them.  Not-for-profit educational\ninstitutions are already struggling to financially recover from the\npandemic, unexpected tax increases can only exacerbate their financial\nwoes. Allowing them to exercise their right to appeal will ensure they\nare able to pursue overturning disadvantageous decisions on tax\nexemptions that they were relying upon when crafting their annual budg-\nets.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere are no fiscal to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
